1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:07,960
They're the center of celebration, the site of great discoveries, spires of power that

2
00:00:07,961 --> 00:00:13,140
spur dreamers to push the limits, symbols that inspire national pride.

3
00:00:14,120 --> 00:00:20,880
Now, the story of visionaries who challenge the heavens to build great towers on Modern

4
00:00:20,881 --> 00:00:21,700
Marvels.

5
00:00:48,411 --> 00:00:53,940
Since the dawn of time, humans have dreamed of breaking the shackles of our landlocked

6
00:00:53,941 --> 00:01:00,780
existence, to soar through the sky and see the world from above.

7
00:01:00,781 --> 00:01:05,980
It can free the spirit from the burdens of life.

8
00:01:05,981 --> 00:01:08,420
Throughout history, people have built towers

9
00:01:08,421 --> 00:01:12,220
to seek the euphoric sensation of height.

10
00:01:12,221 --> 00:01:13,860
When you're happy, when you're satisfied,

11
00:01:13,861 --> 00:01:19,500
when you're in a good mood, you say, well, I'm on top of the world.

12
00:01:19,501 --> 00:01:26,680
The earliest towers were built to bring man closer to God.

13
00:01:27,680 --> 00:01:32,100
Let us build ourselves a city and a tower with its top reaching heaven.

14
00:01:33,300 --> 00:01:36,400
That's how the Bible describes the world's first known tower,

15
00:01:36,960 --> 00:01:40,720
built thousands of years before Christ, the Tower of Babel.

16
00:01:44,160 --> 00:01:47,860
Towers touch more than mankind's fascination with the heavens.

17
00:01:47,861 --> 00:01:51,860
They fulfill a human need for power.

18
00:01:51,861 --> 00:02:00,860
Well, it's in human nature, this demand that he has for power, for domination and being served.

19
00:02:00,861 --> 00:02:09,860
So when you go up a tower and you're in the observatory and looking down, you dominate the area, you dominate the people that are there.

20
00:02:09,861 --> 00:02:16,860
You satisfy, consciously or unconsciously, a need to dominate, a need to be powerful.

21
00:02:16,861 --> 00:02:22,860
One tower illustrates mankind's struggle for power over nature.

22
00:02:22,861 --> 00:02:28,860
The Italians fought the forces of gravity to build the leaning tower of Pisa.

23
00:02:28,861 --> 00:02:37,860
The project began in the year 1173 with plans for a bell tower in Pisa's Cathedral Plaza.

24
00:02:37,861 --> 00:02:44,860
Architects quickly discovered it was a mistake to build a tall, heavy structure on unstable ground.

25
00:02:44,861 --> 00:02:50,860
As soon as construction started, the tower began to sink and lean.

26
00:02:50,861 --> 00:02:57,860
Still, the architects pressed ahead, making structural adjustments as they built.

27
00:02:57,861 --> 00:03:01,860
These changes have kept the towers lean to 5.5 degrees,

28
00:03:01,861 --> 00:03:06,860
though the building continues to sink and is on the verge of falling over.

29
00:03:06,861 --> 00:03:13,860
Right now, an object drop from the top would land 14 feet away from the base.

30
00:03:14,860 --> 00:03:21,860
Ironically, the Tower of Pisa is reputed to be the site of history's greatest discoveries about gravity.

31
00:03:22,860 --> 00:03:28,860
The Italian scientist Galileo theorized all objects fall to earth at an equal speed.

32
00:03:29,860 --> 00:03:34,860
And in the year 1564, it is said he used the tower to prove it.

33
00:03:35,860 --> 00:03:39,360
Galileo dropped cannonballs of different weights.

34
00:03:39,361 --> 00:03:41,860
They fell at exactly the same rate.

35
00:03:44,860 --> 00:03:50,460
Some historians question that this experiment actually did take place,

36
00:03:50,461 --> 00:03:56,160
but the story endures, enhancing Pisa's reputation as a leaning legend.

37
00:03:58,740 --> 00:04:03,560
Other towers have won a place in history because of their deep symbolic importance,

38
00:04:04,160 --> 00:04:08,000
such as the case for London's revered clock tower, Big Ben.

39
00:04:09,380 --> 00:04:13,020
During trying times, Big Ben has been a beacon of strength.

40
00:04:14,860 --> 00:04:21,860
Throughout the Nazi bombing blitz of World War II, Big Ben survived with barely a scratch.

41
00:04:21,861 --> 00:04:28,860
Though much of London was reduced to rubble by relentless German raids, Big Ben towered

42
00:04:28,861 --> 00:04:36,860
above the debris of war as proud a statement of British resolve as the indomitable Winston Churchill himself.

43
00:04:36,861 --> 00:04:46,400
If the British Empire and its Commonwealth last for a thousand years, men will still

44
00:04:46,401 --> 00:04:53,180
say, this was their finest hour.

45
00:04:53,181 --> 00:04:57,420
Big Ben kept nearly perfect time throughout the war.

46
00:04:57,421 --> 00:05:03,480
During one bombing raid, its face was damaged, yet the clock ticked steadily, losing just

47
00:05:03,481 --> 00:05:04,480
one half.

48
00:05:06,860 --> 00:05:13,860
of one second.

49
00:05:13,861 --> 00:05:18,620
Perhaps no building symbolizes the dreams and aspirations of a nation better than the

50
00:05:18,621 --> 00:05:24,800
world's most famous tower, the Eiffel Tower in Paris.

51
00:05:24,801 --> 00:05:30,800
Built in 1889 to commemorate the 100th anniversary of the French Revolution, the Eiffel is a

52
00:05:30,801 --> 00:05:34,500
masterpiece of geometry.

53
00:05:34,501 --> 00:05:39,500
Its 12,000 parts rise to a height of nearly 1,000 feet.

54
00:05:39,501 --> 00:05:43,500
It was the tallest building on Earth for 40 years.

55
00:05:45,500 --> 00:05:50,500
The Eiffel embodies the human drive to reach ever upward.

56
00:05:50,501 --> 00:05:56,500
With the dawn of the Industrial Age, the limits to man's reach knew no bounds.

57
00:05:56,501 --> 00:06:02,500
From one tower to the other, the techniques developed, the engineering developed,

58
00:06:02,501 --> 00:06:08,580
building materials developed and you could build better and bigger towers.

59
00:06:08,581 --> 00:06:18,060
The Eiffel's graceful curves and airy lattice of iron are an inspired blend of art and engineering.

60
00:06:18,061 --> 00:06:23,460
It showed architects throughout the world that when it comes to building towers, anything

61
00:06:23,461 --> 00:06:24,161
is possible.

62
00:06:32,500 --> 00:06:46,500
The 1960s marked a new era in mankind's reach for the heavens, and with the era of space

63
00:06:49,020 --> 00:06:52,580
exploration came the world's first space age tower.

64
00:06:54,260 --> 00:07:00,760
The Seattle Space Needle was the crowning achievement of America's 1962 World's Fair.

65
00:07:00,761 --> 00:07:07,980
It was a building from outer space, a flying saucer frozen in flight.

66
00:07:10,740 --> 00:07:18,460
The Space Needle story is a tale of American ingenuity and optimism, as well as a race against time.

67
00:07:20,820 --> 00:07:26,760
When the Needle was built, the United States was losing a bitter competition for supremacy in space.

68
00:07:26,761 --> 00:07:33,760
The Soviet Union had successfully launched the world's first satellite.

69
00:07:33,761 --> 00:07:40,760
Sputnik was circling the globe every 90 minutes, beaming radio signals to Russia.

70
00:07:40,761 --> 00:07:50,760
The Soviets were first to the moon, with a probe that took fuzzy pictures of ridges and craters so they could receive Russian names.

71
00:07:50,761 --> 00:07:58,160
The space race touched a deep nerve during Vice President Richard Nixon's historic mission to Moscow in 1959.

72
00:07:59,260 --> 00:08:04,860
The Soviet premier told Nixon that communism would triumph in every sphere of life.

73
00:08:07,060 --> 00:08:09,860
Khrushchev, shrewd, aggressive, self-assured,

74
00:08:10,460 --> 00:08:17,860
taunted the Vice President by saying Russia would pass America in seven years and wave to us going by.

75
00:08:20,760 --> 00:08:31,320
The Cold War dominated American life as planners in Seattle prepared for the World's Fair.

76
00:08:32,380 --> 00:08:36,700
The theme for the exhibition would be life in the 21st century.

77
00:08:37,560 --> 00:08:42,904
What better way to project America's optimism about the future than a showcase for U.S.

78
00:08:42,905 --> 00:08:43,605
technology?

79
00:08:43,606 --> 00:08:55,800
And what better way to declare America's desire to win the space race than to capture a UFO and balance it on a slender tripod 60 stories up?

80
00:08:59,060 --> 00:09:02,660
The choice of Seattle for the fair raised some eyebrows.

81
00:09:03,480 --> 00:09:09,740
For much of its history, Seattle was a place for people just passing through on their way to the gold fields of Alaska.

82
00:09:09,741 --> 00:09:17,340
By the 1960s, Seattle was among the world's biggest manufacturers of high-tech jets.

83
00:09:18,120 --> 00:09:21,840
But many people felt the city hadn't truly grown up.

84
00:09:23,540 --> 00:09:29,240
At the time we built the Space Needle, Seattle was a quiet residential city.

85
00:09:29,820 --> 00:09:37,200
Nothing much going on, no major league sports, no cultural things like opera or rock music, anything like that.

86
00:09:37,201 --> 00:09:39,660
and it was just a very quiet place.

87
00:09:39,780 --> 00:09:43,700
Anybody from New York or Los Angeles or San Francisco

88
00:09:43,701 --> 00:09:45,600
would say that this is a dull place.

89
00:09:47,020 --> 00:09:50,100
The World's Fair was a golden opportunity

90
00:09:50,101 --> 00:09:52,300
for international recognition,

91
00:09:52,880 --> 00:09:57,300
a chance for Seattle to join the top tier of American cities.

92
00:09:59,580 --> 00:10:01,620
To construct the Space Needle,

93
00:10:01,720 --> 00:10:05,080
the Fair Commission turned to one of the city's best-known builders,

94
00:10:05,720 --> 00:10:06,640
Howard S. Wright.

95
00:10:07,200 --> 00:10:09,900
They kept saying, you know, we need a theme for the,

96
00:10:09,901 --> 00:10:15,320
we need something, a magnet for this World's Fair.

97
00:10:15,560 --> 00:10:20,540
And they were talking about a space-age 21st century theme.

98
00:10:21,060 --> 00:10:26,860
And this tower, this tower just became suddenly the focal point.

99
00:10:28,100 --> 00:10:33,200
To design the tower, the commission had picked one of Seattle's foremost architects,

100
00:10:33,940 --> 00:10:35,000
John Graham, Jr.

101
00:10:35,001 --> 00:10:40,000
Graham had a reputation for first-of-its-kind designs.

102
00:10:40,001 --> 00:10:44,000
America's first shopping mall opened in 1950,

103
00:10:44,001 --> 00:10:48,000
and the world's first revolving rooftop restaurant

104
00:10:48,001 --> 00:10:51,000
atop an office complex in Hawaii.

105
00:10:51,001 --> 00:10:54,000
With a team of architects,

106
00:10:54,001 --> 00:10:56,000
including Professor Victor Steinbrook

107
00:10:56,001 --> 00:10:58,000
from the University of Washington,

108
00:10:58,001 --> 00:11:03,000
Graham shaped a vision for the world's first space needle.

109
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:08,360
The early designs looked nothing like the finished product,

110
00:11:08,940 --> 00:11:13,140
but the team finally captured the essence of an alien spaceship.

111
00:11:15,440 --> 00:11:20,120
This saucer thing was so hot at that time and so exciting

112
00:11:20,121 --> 00:11:25,220
that the saucer, you know, this elevated, like a UFO,

113
00:11:26,120 --> 00:11:33,320
just became the unanimous and enthusiastic choice of everybody involved.

114
00:11:33,321 --> 00:11:40,320
The Space Needle would be as futuristic as any structure in history.

115
00:11:40,321 --> 00:11:43,320
People would dine to unparalleled views.

116
00:11:43,321 --> 00:11:47,320
As an attraction for the fair, it would be irresistible.

117
00:11:47,321 --> 00:11:53,320
Absolutely, I mean that blew people away seeing this 500 foot tower just sitting there.

118
00:11:53,321 --> 00:11:58,320
I mean it was something that was thrilling and a lot of people said,

119
00:11:58,321 --> 00:12:02,320
I don't think they're going to pull it off and we didn't know we were going to pull it off.

120
00:12:03,320 --> 00:12:04,720
The problem was this.

121
00:12:05,360 --> 00:12:11,040
The World's Fair was set to open in April of 1962, just 18 months away.

122
00:12:11,600 --> 00:12:14,400
And the Space Needle was just a pretty picture.

123
00:12:14,960 --> 00:12:17,200
No blueprints, no financing.

124
00:12:18,120 --> 00:12:24,440
It would be an unprecedented architectural statement, if it could be built in time.

125
00:12:26,280 --> 00:12:32,120
The Space Needle's architect did over 200 sketches before arriving at his final design.

126
00:12:33,320 --> 00:12:46,400
With 18 months to go, construction was well underway for Seattle's Space Age Fair.

127
00:12:47,040 --> 00:12:51,200
But the space needle was still just an architect's dream.

128
00:12:52,320 --> 00:12:58,700
The Fair Commission could not secure government financing, and private investors were holding back.

129
00:12:58,860 --> 00:13:01,060
We didn't know if the property would make money.

130
00:13:01,061 --> 00:13:03,440
and we were spending $4.5 million,

131
00:13:04,040 --> 00:13:07,280
and in those days, $4.5 million was a lot of money.

132
00:13:08,160 --> 00:13:10,380
We didn't know if people would pay a dollar or a ride

133
00:13:10,381 --> 00:13:15,060
to come up there and look out and see those beautiful views.

134
00:13:16,620 --> 00:13:20,220
Meanwhile, structural engineers were confronting a different problem.

135
00:13:21,080 --> 00:13:24,100
To achieve the floating effect for the top house,

136
00:13:24,680 --> 00:13:27,120
the Needle's legs had to be toothpick thin.

137
00:13:27,121 --> 00:13:35,120
With a restaurant, observation deck and kitchen 60 stories in the air, the design was top-heavy,

138
00:13:35,121 --> 00:13:41,820
weighing nearly 6,000 tons. A stiff gust of wind could cause the building to sway

139
00:13:41,821 --> 00:13:44,010
dangerously back and forth.

140
00:13:44,011 --> 00:13:49,120
To solve this problem, engineers designed a massive foundation,

141
00:13:49,121 --> 00:13:52,095
weighing as much as the legs and top combined.

142
00:13:52,096 --> 00:13:55,200
This brought the center of gravity to just a few

143
00:13:55,201 --> 00:13:56,660
feet above the ground.

144
00:13:59,180 --> 00:14:05,600
Building the foundation began in April 1961, when financing was finally secured.

145
00:14:06,660 --> 00:14:11,800
It was exactly one year and four days before the World's Fair was scheduled to open.

146
00:14:14,240 --> 00:14:20,440
The foundation was 30 feet deep, filled with 250 tons of reinforcing steel.

147
00:14:20,441 --> 00:14:29,240
Huge anchor bolts were installed so the needle could literally be bolted to the giant concrete base.

148
00:14:30,240 --> 00:14:31,740
Then came cement.

149
00:14:33,180 --> 00:14:41,720
467 truckloads poured nonstop for 12 straight hours, the largest continuous pour ever attempted in the western U.S.

150
00:14:41,721 --> 00:14:51,560
as the foundation took shape work was underway on the girders that would form the space needle's legs

151
00:14:51,561 --> 00:15:01,520
the genius of building with steel was that the entire structure could be prefabricated

152
00:15:01,521 --> 00:15:04,860
at the pacific car and foundry company in suburban seattle

153
00:15:04,861 --> 00:15:14,200
three shifts worked round the clock bending and welding to create easy to assemble components

154
00:15:14,201 --> 00:15:20,760
part by part the building was trucked to the fairgrounds for erection

155
00:15:20,761 --> 00:15:26,360
as the first components went up workers at the plant stayed one step ahead

156
00:15:26,361 --> 00:15:31,920
there was so little time to build the needle that nothing was left a chance

157
00:15:32,860 --> 00:15:38,300
Every steel coupling was tested in the factory to be sure they fit perfectly in the field.

158
00:15:42,460 --> 00:15:45,200
The precision craftsmanship paid off.

159
00:15:45,680 --> 00:15:49,980
The needle rose with incredible speed, 120 feet per month.

160
00:15:50,720 --> 00:15:56,500
It was one of the fastest construction jobs in history, and one of the most dangerous.

161
00:15:57,260 --> 00:16:01,840
It was a lot more dangerous because frequently we're working with nothing under us.

162
00:16:01,920 --> 00:16:07,680
but air the people that were working at one particular point for a long period of time

163
00:16:07,681 --> 00:16:13,200
like the people putting in bolts or the people welding had safety belts and would tie off but

164
00:16:13,201 --> 00:16:20,880
the connectors had to constantly be moving climbing around and dodging things so it was a

165
00:16:21,680 --> 00:16:26,000
good idea not to be tied off to something because you might have to run like hell in a minute

166
00:16:26,001 --> 00:16:35,000
Connecting the huge leg sections was by far the riskiest assignment.

167
00:16:35,001 --> 00:16:40,000
Each piece was 90 feet long and weighed 47 tons.

168
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:49,000
Workers crawled inside the framework to bolt and weld these pieces together.

169
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:55,000
Lifting began early each morning when winds were calmest.

170
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:58,820
On some days, though, nature did not cooperate.

171
00:16:59,960 --> 00:17:02,120
We had a bad windstorm one day,

172
00:17:02,121 --> 00:17:06,220
and the top was going like this so severely

173
00:17:06,221 --> 00:17:08,840
that we had a piece of iron hanging on the hook

174
00:17:08,841 --> 00:17:12,640
that we could not control because of all this motion.

175
00:17:12,800 --> 00:17:15,240
So we had to just lower it down into a triangle

176
00:17:15,241 --> 00:17:16,820
that was formed by some of the members,

177
00:17:17,040 --> 00:17:18,780
and it just banged around in there

178
00:17:18,781 --> 00:17:21,940
like a big dinner bell for several hours.

179
00:17:21,941 --> 00:17:26,320
Nothing like the Space Needle had ever been built.

180
00:17:27,120 --> 00:17:32,160
Its precarious footholds challenged even the most seasoned construction workers.

181
00:17:34,280 --> 00:17:36,260
Oh, God, it scared the wits out of you.

182
00:17:36,540 --> 00:17:40,120
I'm used to going up in tall buildings and being in construction elevators,

183
00:17:40,340 --> 00:17:42,880
but, yeah, it scared the wits out of me.

184
00:17:44,620 --> 00:17:47,040
Hello, test 1234. Testing.

185
00:17:47,460 --> 00:17:49,760
Oh, excuse me. We're on.

186
00:17:49,761 --> 00:17:52,560
with us right now to my immediate right is...

187
00:17:52,561 --> 00:17:54,680
As the needle rose higher and higher,

188
00:17:55,100 --> 00:17:56,940
so did public enthusiasm.

189
00:17:57,780 --> 00:18:00,060
There were nightly updates on the TV news.

190
00:18:00,860 --> 00:18:03,040
There were publicity stunts, too,

191
00:18:03,420 --> 00:18:06,080
like the catered meal to show how the restaurant floor

192
00:18:06,081 --> 00:18:08,860
would revolve on specially designed tracks.

193
00:18:10,800 --> 00:18:14,300
And the woman who wove a space needle into her hair.

194
00:18:16,620 --> 00:18:18,860
Even the steely-nerved iron workers

195
00:18:18,861 --> 00:18:22,860
took a break to pose for photographers.

196
00:18:22,861 --> 00:18:29,220
Most of us had a sense that this was going to be a symbol of the city or an important

197
00:18:29,221 --> 00:18:31,220
symbol of some sort.

198
00:18:31,221 --> 00:18:36,040
Probably most of us would say, if asked then, that it's just a job, but I don't think it

199
00:18:36,041 --> 00:18:37,040
was.

200
00:18:37,041 --> 00:18:40,280
I think this is a special job for most of us.

201
00:18:40,281 --> 00:18:42,240
I want to tell you, it was spectacular.

202
00:18:42,241 --> 00:18:47,860
We didn't have a 60-story building in Seattle, and no one knew how high up in the air that

203
00:18:47,861 --> 00:18:48,860
was.

204
00:18:48,861 --> 00:18:54,620
all uh everyone in the city was looking and thinking my god i wonder where it's going to all

205
00:18:54,621 --> 00:19:02,860
end up folks found out in just six months the legs were complete and men were already working on the

206
00:19:02,861 --> 00:19:12,140
top work here went just as quickly everything was pre-fabricated for speedy assembly

207
00:19:12,141 --> 00:19:19,400
just nine months after groundbreaking the space needle was nearing completion

208
00:19:19,401 --> 00:19:25,760
the final piece would be the beacon to warn airplanes 605 feet up

209
00:19:25,761 --> 00:19:34,920
remarkably despite the danger there were no serious injuries or deaths building was on time

210
00:19:34,921 --> 00:19:42,080
on budget and out of this world we rode up in the construction elevator at about nine

211
00:19:42,081 --> 00:19:45,740
o'clock at night and the wind was blowing a little bit. As we stepped out

212
00:19:45,741 --> 00:19:52,280
there and looked around I want to tell you you got a feeling of exuberance and

213
00:19:52,281 --> 00:19:57,620
of achievement to actually stand up there and look out upon this beautiful

214
00:19:57,621 --> 00:20:04,400
city all this twinkling lights below. It was just something that I'd never

215
00:20:04,401 --> 00:20:08,080
experienced before in my life.

216
00:20:12,080 --> 00:20:20,080
For hundreds of thousands of visitors, this is how it will begin.

217
00:20:20,081 --> 00:20:24,080
An elevated ride on a one-track train, a monorail it is called.

218
00:20:24,081 --> 00:20:31,080
And the passengers who ride it are heading out of this world to a distant time and a future century.

219
00:20:31,081 --> 00:20:38,080
The Seattle Exposition was America's first World's Fair in 22 years.

220
00:20:38,081 --> 00:20:42,480
and it was a fantastic glimpse at the world of tomorrow.

221
00:20:44,040 --> 00:20:46,340
Here were the newest television sets,

222
00:20:47,080 --> 00:20:48,220
satellites and rockets,

223
00:20:48,860 --> 00:20:51,880
and a replica of America's first spacecraft.

224
00:20:53,980 --> 00:20:57,000
People were even thrilled by a new machine

225
00:20:57,001 --> 00:20:59,180
that made change for a dollar bill.

226
00:21:01,920 --> 00:21:04,860
The biggest attraction by far was the needle.

227
00:21:04,861 --> 00:21:12,560
20,000 people lined up at the base every day to pay the $1 entry fee.

228
00:21:13,940 --> 00:21:19,980
The 43-second ride in the space capsule elevator was an adventure nobody wanted to miss.

229
00:21:21,420 --> 00:21:25,720
Once on top, the panorama was simply thrilling.

230
00:21:34,860 --> 00:21:44,900
The most novel part of the Space Needle experience was the revolving restaurant, offering a romantic

231
00:21:44,901 --> 00:21:46,780
atmosphere high above the city.

232
00:21:49,580 --> 00:21:58,280
The floor revolves 360 degrees in an hour, and the 90-ton turntable is so finely balanced

233
00:21:58,281 --> 00:22:02,620
it takes only a one-horsepower motor to make the world go round.

234
00:22:02,621 --> 00:22:08,140
The ride is so smooth you can balance a cigarette on its end.

235
00:22:10,380 --> 00:22:13,640
Reporters were quick to spot one potential problem.

236
00:22:15,060 --> 00:22:20,120
I've been wondering with the stationary kitchen and several hundred rotating guests,

237
00:22:20,240 --> 00:22:22,300
how in the world does a waitress find your customers?

238
00:22:22,820 --> 00:22:27,740
Well, it does present some problems, but we've worked in a clock-like device

239
00:22:27,741 --> 00:22:31,100
at each door leading from the kitchen,

240
00:22:31,101 --> 00:22:33,500
which indicates just exactly where,

241
00:22:33,700 --> 00:22:36,200
what portion of the rotating platform is at that time.

242
00:22:37,300 --> 00:22:40,360
The Seattle Space Needle was an instant hit.

243
00:22:40,940 --> 00:22:43,820
It became the most recognizable symbol of the fair.

244
00:22:44,700 --> 00:22:47,960
The post office even issued a special edition stamp.

245
00:22:49,860 --> 00:22:53,300
The needle was a triumph that reflected the civic spirit

246
00:22:53,301 --> 00:22:54,980
of a proud community.

247
00:22:54,981 --> 00:22:59,980
Its colorful saucer was a bold addition to the Seattle skyline.

248
00:22:59,981 --> 00:23:03,980
The needle brought another distinction to Seattle.

249
00:23:03,981 --> 00:23:07,980
It was the tallest building in the American West.

250
00:23:07,981 --> 00:23:14,980
However, the enthusiasm it sparked would soon prompt other cities to build even taller,

251
00:23:14,981 --> 00:23:19,980
including one tower that would become the tallest building on Earth.

252
00:23:19,981 --> 00:23:29,580
The Space Needle is built to a very high standard, double the building code requirements for earthquake and wind resistance.

253
00:23:37,020 --> 00:23:43,140
May 1st, 1931, opening day for the newest addition to the New York skyline.

254
00:23:43,141 --> 00:23:45,900
The tallest building in the world is open for the first time.

255
00:23:45,901 --> 00:23:51,240
The Empire State Building constructed at a cost of 77 million dollars after 14 months of work.

256
00:23:51,600 --> 00:23:57,920
It is the children who have been selected to cut the ribbons that will mark the official opening of the 102-story office building.

257
00:24:02,300 --> 00:24:07,100
In the 1930s as the skyscraper became a symbol of power and might,

258
00:24:07,480 --> 00:24:12,301
New York City proudly became home to the world's tallest building.

259
00:24:12,302 --> 00:24:14,420
It created quite a sensation.

260
00:24:15,900 --> 00:24:25,220
The skyscraper craze intensified in the 1970s when several American buildings laid claim

261
00:24:25,221 --> 00:24:27,000
to the title world's tallest.

262
00:24:27,740 --> 00:24:30,820
However, Canada captured the top prize.

263
00:24:32,060 --> 00:24:39,520
The CN Tower in Toronto built in 1976 by the CN, the Canadian National Railroad, is still

264
00:24:39,521 --> 00:24:41,140
the tallest building on earth.

265
00:24:41,141 --> 00:24:50,140
At 1,815 feet, the CN Tower is 181 stories high.

266
00:24:59,320 --> 00:25:04,300
The CN Tower was part of an effort to restore Toronto's lakefront to the people.

267
00:25:05,180 --> 00:25:11,080
For generations, the shores of Lake Ontario had been a place to escape the dog days of summer.

268
00:25:11,140 --> 00:25:18,140
By the 1960s, though, the coastline was a working waterfront of harbors and industry,

269
00:25:18,141 --> 00:25:25,140
and the sprawling switchyards of the CN Railroad was a barrier that separated downtown from the lake.

270
00:25:29,140 --> 00:25:36,140
In 1968, CN unveiled a plan to transform the rail yards into a showcase of urban renewal.

271
00:25:36,141 --> 00:25:45,520
renewal. The project included a towering television antenna. The huge development

272
00:25:45,521 --> 00:25:50,580
was intended to revitalize the city's lakefront and the antenna would improve

273
00:25:50,581 --> 00:25:57,320
TV reception. By 1968 the city's high-rise office buildings were blocking

274
00:25:57,321 --> 00:26:02,900
reception for thousands of homes. Back when I was a kid here in Toronto watching

275
00:26:02,901 --> 00:26:06,020
a hockey game on Saturday nights you couldn't tell how many players there were

276
00:26:06,021 --> 00:26:10,820
on the ice sometimes because of the ghosts and the you know those kind of images so i think

277
00:26:10,821 --> 00:26:18,420
everybody here was very grateful when tv reception took a big jump amazingly the architect picked for

278
00:26:18,421 --> 00:26:25,380
the job had never designed a tower in his life ned baldwin had built a ship terminal in miami

279
00:26:26,020 --> 00:26:33,220
and the harvard university school of design but nothing of notable height we were all naive

280
00:26:33,221 --> 00:26:41,300
I mean, I was a 36-year-old kid, and I'd never built anything more than six stories high,

281
00:26:41,560 --> 00:26:46,700
and when I sketched the original concepts as to what we were going to do,

282
00:26:46,760 --> 00:26:47,800
I just couldn't believe it.

283
00:26:47,920 --> 00:26:50,900
You know, it was just, the amount to learn was astronomical.

284
00:26:53,200 --> 00:26:58,720
Baldwin's first decision was to scrap the three-masted concept which early planners envisioned.

285
00:26:59,280 --> 00:27:00,580
It would cost too much.

286
00:27:01,480 --> 00:27:07,080
Instead, he proposed a sweeping spire with a seven-story ring for broadcast equipment,

287
00:27:07,420 --> 00:27:09,560
a restaurant, and observation decks.

288
00:27:10,500 --> 00:27:18,460
Well, my preoccupation from day one from a design standpoint was that the tower be a graceful addition to the skyline,

289
00:27:18,720 --> 00:27:19,860
that it looked good in silhouette.

290
00:27:20,220 --> 00:27:21,520
It had to look good in silhouette.

291
00:27:21,660 --> 00:27:25,740
That's where most people experience it, and that's where it was going to be a symbol of Toronto.

292
00:27:26,400 --> 00:27:28,240
You know, I knew it was going to be a symbol of Toronto.

293
00:27:28,340 --> 00:27:29,140
How could it not be?

294
00:27:30,580 --> 00:27:34,460
The decision to build came from CN President Norman McMillan.

295
00:27:35,200 --> 00:27:40,380
To jumpstart construction, he essentially wrote a blank check for $25 million.

296
00:27:41,840 --> 00:27:47,420
Much of the building wasn't even designed when excavation began in 1973.

297
00:27:48,240 --> 00:27:50,380
It was a totally reckless and outrageous thing.

298
00:27:51,120 --> 00:27:57,380
It shows the strength of McMillan that he was able to persuade the board to adopt the decision.

299
00:27:57,381 --> 00:28:04,380
And it's an amazing decision because once you kick this thing off and get the ball rolling and the shovel in the ground, there's no stopping.

300
00:28:04,540 --> 00:28:07,440
It would be a total embarrassment to stop. I mean, you can't stop.

301
00:28:08,760 --> 00:28:14,960
What Macmillan may have foreseen was the coming demise of CN's master plan for the rail yards.

302
00:28:16,180 --> 00:28:19,280
City officials complained it would create too much traffic.

303
00:28:20,420 --> 00:28:23,200
But by then, the tower was well on its way.

304
00:28:23,201 --> 00:28:32,240
The 1,500-foot concrete portion was built like a hollow chimney by an ingenious device called a slip form.

305
00:28:34,480 --> 00:28:40,860
This wooden mold was constantly on the move, inching upward at the rate of 22 feet per day.

306
00:28:42,900 --> 00:28:48,980
Workmen continuously fed cement into the form bucket by bucket, day and night, for months.

307
00:28:48,981 --> 00:28:56,160
As the slip form pushed up day by day, the CN Tower raced toward the sky.

308
00:28:58,540 --> 00:29:03,840
The whole city here, though, was electrified by it, and people watched it with great interest daily.

309
00:29:04,380 --> 00:29:10,620
It gave us a real sense, perhaps for the first time, that Toronto would and could be a world-class city,

310
00:29:10,700 --> 00:29:13,420
and that we should be proud of ourselves just on that basis.

311
00:29:13,421 --> 00:29:19,960
The CN Tower did not set out to become the world's tallest building,

312
00:29:19,961 --> 00:29:23,680
but designers continued to work while construction was underway

313
00:29:23,681 --> 00:29:27,160
and discovered the record was within reach.

314
00:29:28,400 --> 00:29:33,960
The tourist attraction function became more set in people's minds

315
00:29:33,961 --> 00:29:36,760
that, you know, we are building a tourist attraction first

316
00:29:36,761 --> 00:29:39,400
and a broadcast antenna second.

317
00:29:39,860 --> 00:29:43,400
And everybody sort of woke up to the fact that by adding a few feet,

318
00:29:43,420 --> 00:29:46,420
we would be the tallest in the world.

319
00:29:46,421 --> 00:29:49,020
Workers added 40 feet of cement,

320
00:29:49,021 --> 00:29:55,020
and on February 22, 1974, the concrete crew topped out.

321
00:30:02,720 --> 00:30:05,220
Work was far from finished, however.

322
00:30:05,221 --> 00:30:08,320
Because of its great height, the CN Tower was bombarded

323
00:30:08,321 --> 00:30:11,920
by Canada's notorious winter winds,

324
00:30:11,921 --> 00:30:14,220
causing the building to wobble and bend.

325
00:30:15,220 --> 00:30:18,660
This could be disastrous for a concrete structure

326
00:30:18,661 --> 00:30:21,700
because flexing causes concrete to crack.

327
00:30:23,400 --> 00:30:25,660
Engineers anticipated this problem

328
00:30:25,661 --> 00:30:29,780
and designed a sophisticated network of steel-reinforcing cables.

329
00:30:30,700 --> 00:30:33,460
When the cables were tightened, it would stiffen the building.

330
00:30:34,660 --> 00:30:36,900
A wind blast of 200 miles per hour

331
00:30:36,901 --> 00:30:40,820
would move the concrete top just an inch and a half.

332
00:30:40,821 --> 00:30:49,620
The cables were carefully threaded throughout the tower, 80 miles of cable in all.

333
00:30:51,380 --> 00:30:57,580
Meantime, the 400-ton support for the SkyPod was slowly lifted by hydraulic jacks.

334
00:30:59,720 --> 00:31:05,700
The pod became the most expensive part of the job because everything had to come up by crane.

335
00:31:05,701 --> 00:31:15,700
As the pod took shape, more and more features were added, shooting the $25 million price tag up to $63 million.

336
00:31:19,700 --> 00:31:26,360
By far, the most acrobatic work was yet to come, installation of the 300-foot antenna.

337
00:31:28,180 --> 00:31:35,600
A giant Sikorsky helicopter nicknamed Olga lifted the steel sections one by one.

338
00:31:35,700 --> 00:31:49,300
Daredevil workers then joined the parts together as the helicopter hovered overhead.

339
00:31:49,301 --> 00:31:54,800
The final section was ready on April 2, 1975.

340
00:31:54,801 --> 00:31:59,220
This piece of Toronto history was given a special send off.

341
00:31:59,221 --> 00:32:04,600
The tip was the most difficult section to attach.

342
00:32:04,601 --> 00:32:07,900
were aiming for a target just a few feet wide.

343
00:32:12,280 --> 00:32:15,820
After several attempts, success.

344
00:32:19,500 --> 00:32:23,960
The CN Tower set a record that has lasted more than 20 years,

345
00:32:23,961 --> 00:32:27,540
the tallest freestanding structure ever built.

346
00:32:28,800 --> 00:32:31,240
Like a flag atop Mount Everest,

347
00:32:31,241 --> 00:32:34,540
the Canadian Maple Leaf flew proudly this day

348
00:32:34,640 --> 00:32:36,340
above the streets of Toronto.

349
00:32:39,580 --> 00:32:42,720
The CN Tower has become one of Toronto's top tourist

350
00:32:42,820 --> 00:32:44,960
attractions.

351
00:32:45,060 --> 00:32:48,820
Elevators whiz to the top at the rate of 20 feet per second,

352
00:32:48,920 --> 00:32:53,900
the same speed a jet aircraft gains altitude after takeoff.

353
00:32:54,000 --> 00:32:57,500
The view from the Skypod is spellbinding.

354
00:32:57,600 --> 00:33:00,900
People here just love to look out the window.

355
00:33:00,901 --> 00:33:02,940
from as young as 18 months old, little babies,

356
00:33:03,040 --> 00:33:05,600
press their faces against the window and just stare in awe

357
00:33:05,700 --> 00:33:07,740
at being able to get as high in the sky as they can.

358
00:33:09,240 --> 00:33:12,780
The tower also offers a different kind of view

359
00:33:13,080 --> 00:33:15,040
from a unique glass floor.

360
00:33:16,740 --> 00:33:20,360
It's 114 stories from here to the ground.

361
00:33:20,820 --> 00:33:25,220
The experience on the glass floor is like walking on air.

362
00:33:25,221 --> 00:33:37,020
The tower offers an even loftier perch at a second level another 33 floors up.

363
00:33:37,021 --> 00:33:45,220
The space deck is the world's highest public observation gallery.

364
00:33:45,221 --> 00:33:50,920
Every year 1.7 million visitors come to the CN Tower.

365
00:33:50,921 --> 00:33:54,900
People love to peer at the world from on high.

366
00:33:54,901 --> 00:33:57,440
Officials have been adding attractions to the tower,

367
00:33:57,880 --> 00:34:03,580
including elaborate virtual reality rides to draw people back again and again.

368
00:34:05,120 --> 00:34:09,160
We have set about in the last four years to deliver more things,

369
00:34:09,340 --> 00:34:11,820
both at the base of the tower and the top of the tower,

370
00:34:11,960 --> 00:34:17,940
to make a visit worthwhile rather than just been there, done that kind of a thing as a tourist attraction.

371
00:34:17,941 --> 00:34:25,280
High-flying entertainment has become a driving force behind many of the world's tall towers,

372
00:34:25,560 --> 00:34:30,060
and one facility in particular has taken the concept to new heights.

373
00:34:30,940 --> 00:34:35,060
The world's newest tower offers unbelievable thrills.

374
00:34:36,540 --> 00:34:40,960
Las Vegas has launched tall towers into the stratosphere.

375
00:34:59,169 --> 00:35:03,960
The CN Tower deviates only one-tenth of an inch from being perfectly vertical.

376
00:35:03,961 --> 00:35:10,820
America's insatiable appetite for entertainment has fueled an explosion of amusement centers and theme parks.

377
00:35:11,760 --> 00:35:17,300
And with every passing year, there's a new attraction striving to create the ultimate in family fun.

378
00:35:20,620 --> 00:35:26,860
This focus on the family has even transformed America's gambling capital, Las Vegas.

379
00:35:29,500 --> 00:35:33,560
Years ago, this was a town known for its neon glitz.

380
00:35:33,960 --> 00:35:41,200
But things began to change around 1990.

381
00:35:41,201 --> 00:35:47,680
Now the famed Las Vegas Strip is a place of magic castles, pirate ships and a waterfall

382
00:35:47,681 --> 00:35:55,360
that erupts into a fiery volcano.

383
00:35:55,361 --> 00:36:01,200
Casino owner Bob Stupac knew that he too would have to build an attraction to keep his customers

384
00:36:01,201 --> 00:36:02,200
coming.

385
00:36:02,201 --> 00:36:08,720
Stupec was a high-stakes Las Vegas legend, famous for winning a million-dollar bet on the Super Bowl.

386
00:36:09,440 --> 00:36:13,920
But his idea for a roadside attraction would become his biggest gamble,

387
00:36:14,800 --> 00:36:18,120
a tower like nothing that had ever been built before.

388
00:36:19,800 --> 00:36:25,060
Because it's Las Vegas, it's either going to be brighter, it's going to be louder, it's going to be higher,

389
00:36:25,360 --> 00:36:29,400
it's going to be more dramatic, it's going to be practically any adjective that you can apply

390
00:36:29,401 --> 00:36:34,440
in the sense of an attraction and how it's going to react to people.

391
00:36:34,540 --> 00:36:35,640
It has to be a draw.

392
00:36:36,940 --> 00:36:40,840
The stratosphere would not become the world's tallest tower,

393
00:36:41,080 --> 00:36:44,400
but its upper pod would become the world's biggest.

394
00:36:45,180 --> 00:36:51,280
Twelve stories of banquet rooms, a cocktail bar, and observation platforms.

395
00:36:52,080 --> 00:36:58,240
100,000 square feet of floor space in a shell shaped like a precious gem.

396
00:36:58,241 --> 00:37:02,760
I think the tower really represents a diamond in the desert.

397
00:37:04,020 --> 00:37:08,500
The way the legs come apart at the 720-foot level

398
00:37:08,501 --> 00:37:12,080
and kind of transform into a jeweler setting, if you will,

399
00:37:12,540 --> 00:37:16,440
and then with all the glass and the sparkling lights that we have on it,

400
00:37:16,520 --> 00:37:19,980
sitting in that jeweler setting kind of form the diamond in my eyes.

401
00:37:22,480 --> 00:37:27,660
Bob Stupac tried to squeeze as many attractions into the tower as possible.

402
00:37:28,240 --> 00:37:35,320
An entire floor is dedicated to wedding chapels, where couples can literally create a match made in heaven.

403
00:37:37,880 --> 00:37:42,840
To shorten the waiting time for visitors, special double-decker elevators were built.

404
00:37:43,820 --> 00:37:48,220
The elevator is actually two compartments stacked inside the same shaft.

405
00:37:49,600 --> 00:37:55,840
Monitors inside the lower car help the operator know what's happening in the compartment above.

406
00:37:58,240 --> 00:38:06,620
At one point during construction, Bob Stupak proposed an 800-foot addition, so the Stratosphere

407
00:38:06,621 --> 00:38:10,400
would surpass Toronto's CN Tower by 10 feet.

408
00:38:11,060 --> 00:38:16,260
The plan was scrapped when airport officials complained it would be a hazard for planes.

409
00:38:17,740 --> 00:38:24,060
The next wild idea came from Stratosphere consultant Ned Baldwin, who suggested a carnival

410
00:38:24,061 --> 00:38:26,960
ride of swinging chairs perched on top of the tower.

411
00:38:26,961 --> 00:38:30,660
You have to have some function for this spike on the top.

412
00:38:31,360 --> 00:38:38,220
And so I just looked at it and I thought, well, you know, there are theme park rides that look very much like that spike we want.

413
00:38:38,640 --> 00:38:40,320
And so why not put one up there?

414
00:38:42,220 --> 00:38:44,960
Baldwin's spinning chair concept turned out to be impractical.

415
00:38:45,740 --> 00:38:49,440
But his idea for a high-flying ride took off.

416
00:38:49,441 --> 00:39:01,480
The stratosphere is home to the Big Shot, which uses compressed air to shoot 16 passengers

417
00:39:01,481 --> 00:39:04,240
into space as quick as a rocket.

418
00:39:04,241 --> 00:39:18,241
It takes two and a half seconds to travel 160 feet straight up.

419
00:39:21,240 --> 00:39:29,240
For a moment, as the big shot slows near its summit, riders are actually weightless.

420
00:39:29,241 --> 00:39:32,740
Then they're shot back toward Earth for a bungee-style bounce.

421
00:39:36,000 --> 00:39:43,740
Riders experience 4Gs, four times the force of gravity, as they're propelled toward the stars.

422
00:39:47,980 --> 00:39:51,880
The stratosphere's second source for thrills is the high roller.

423
00:39:51,881 --> 00:39:58,880
The world's highest roller coaster whips around the tower on 865 feet of track.

424
00:39:58,881 --> 00:40:05,880
It's not as fast as coasters on the ground, but the view leaves people breathless.

425
00:40:05,881 --> 00:40:06,600
breathless.

426
00:40:13,740 --> 00:40:17,920
The stratosphere is by far the tallest building in Las Vegas,

427
00:40:18,420 --> 00:40:21,740
reaching upward 1,149 feet.

428
00:40:23,300 --> 00:40:26,300
Bob Stupac was unable to finish the building alone.

429
00:40:27,040 --> 00:40:29,460
Grand casinos took over to complete the tower

430
00:40:29,461 --> 00:40:31,380
and renovate the hotel at its base.

431
00:40:31,381 --> 00:40:39,160
But Stupak's statue stands at the entrance in a characteristic Las Vegas pose.

432
00:40:41,560 --> 00:40:47,240
Although this did not become the world's tallest building, it will be difficult for others to top

433
00:40:47,241 --> 00:40:48,561
rides like these.

434
00:40:48,562 --> 00:40:54,780
The stratosphere underscores how each of the world's great towers has a distinct

435
00:40:54,781 --> 00:41:00,820
personality. They're reflections of the people who built them and the eras that

436
00:41:00,821 --> 00:41:06,800
gave them birth and as each tower ages it can grow to become an integral part of

437
00:41:06,801 --> 00:41:13,160
its community. Throughout the world they've become the sites of great

438
00:41:13,161 --> 00:41:21,120
celebration and death-defying stunts. We'll see what the Space Needle CN Tower

439
00:41:21,121 --> 00:41:26,120
and stratosphere have inspired since their completion.

440
00:41:26,121 --> 00:41:29,120
The elevator ride to the top of the stratosphere tower

441
00:41:29,121 --> 00:41:31,120
takes a mere 30 seconds.

442
00:41:40,120 --> 00:41:44,120
There's an old saying that architecture is like frozen music,

443
00:41:44,121 --> 00:41:47,120
a symphony of vision, perseverance,

444
00:41:47,121 --> 00:41:49,120
and technological achievement.

445
00:41:51,120 --> 00:41:57,840
The world's tall towers are certainly that, but their history has been anything but frozen in time.

446
00:41:59,280 --> 00:42:02,160
The Space Needle marked a turning point for Seattle.

447
00:42:03,200 --> 00:42:10,240
This quiet, low-rise city of 1962 has blossomed into a bustling, modern metropolis.

448
00:42:13,360 --> 00:42:17,600
The Needle's original colors have given way to a more modern hue,

449
00:42:17,601 --> 00:42:22,860
but the building still stands out amid the city's profile of glass and steel.

450
00:42:23,980 --> 00:42:28,480
And on special occasions, the needle is impossible to miss.

451
00:42:31,120 --> 00:42:35,320
Perhaps the biggest sensation at the needle came in its very first year,

452
00:42:35,540 --> 00:42:38,100
when Elvis Presley came to shoot a movie.

453
00:42:39,740 --> 00:42:43,140
Elvis falls in love during his visit to Seattle in the film

454
00:42:43,141 --> 00:42:45,040
It Happened at the World's Fair.

455
00:42:47,600 --> 00:42:56,160
In Toronto, the urban renewal that the CN Tower was intended to create has slowly taken root.

456
00:42:57,160 --> 00:43:02,420
The lakefront rail yards are giving way to convention facilities and public parks.

457
00:43:05,380 --> 00:43:09,640
A fellow modern marvel has been built at the foot of the CN Tower,

458
00:43:09,641 --> 00:43:13,980
a massive retractable roof stadium called the Sky Dome.

459
00:43:13,981 --> 00:43:21,340
Because of its great height, the CN Tower has been a magnet for daredevils.

460
00:43:22,100 --> 00:43:27,560
People have climbed the outside and pogo-jumped its 2,570 steps.

461
00:43:31,000 --> 00:43:35,240
To get down, a television stuntman tried the free-fall approach.

462
00:43:43,980 --> 00:43:46,920
At the stratosphere, officials are constantly looking

463
00:43:47,020 --> 00:43:51,160
for novel ways to increase the tower's excitement.

464
00:43:51,260 --> 00:43:53,860
Some of the proposals have been so fanciful,

465
00:43:53,960 --> 00:43:57,060
it's uncertain they'll ever be built.

466
00:43:57,160 --> 00:44:00,500
King Kong will be about a 70-foot gorilla.

467
00:44:00,600 --> 00:44:03,200
There will actually be a ride that about 40 people can get

468
00:44:03,300 --> 00:44:07,400
into, and King Kong will then ascend the tower

469
00:44:07,500 --> 00:44:10,280
as he climbs up growling and snarling and waving

470
00:44:10,380 --> 00:44:12,880
to all his fans on the ground.

471
00:44:12,881 --> 00:44:16,580
The excitement that tall towers create continues to drive

472
00:44:16,680 --> 00:44:20,180
architects and builders throughout the world.

473
00:44:20,280 --> 00:44:23,820
Barcelona, Moscow, Montreal, Sydney,

474
00:44:23,920 --> 00:44:27,420
Seoul, Beijing, Berlin.

475
00:44:27,520 --> 00:44:32,440
Towers grace the skyline in many of the world's largest cities.

476
00:44:32,540 --> 00:44:36,040
Architects are planning new ones every day.

477
00:44:36,140 --> 00:44:39,880
Human nature will be in a hundred years what it is today

478
00:44:39,980 --> 00:44:42,080
and what it was centuries ago.

479
00:44:42,081 --> 00:44:45,080
so people will want to reach new heights.

480
00:44:45,081 --> 00:44:49,080
The construction of towers will always require

481
00:44:49,081 --> 00:44:52,080
courageous visionaries who are willing to build

482
00:44:52,081 --> 00:44:55,080
in the face of uncertainty.

483
00:44:55,081 --> 00:44:59,080
But the finished product can stir such deep emotions.

484
00:44:59,081 --> 00:45:03,080
There will always be people willing to take the chance.

485
00:45:03,081 --> 00:45:06,080
As the poet Robert Browning wrote a century ago,

486
00:45:06,081 --> 00:45:10,080
man's reach should exceed his grasp.

487
00:45:10,081 --> 00:45:12,320
Oh, what's a heaven for?

